---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PlaceholderPlate from "../../components/PlaceholderPlate.astro";
import { SITE } from "../../consts";
import { breadcrumbSchema } from "../../lib/schema";

const projects = (await getCollection("interiorProjects")).sort((a, b) => b.data.year - a.data.year);

const jsonLd = breadcrumbSchema([
  { name: "Home", path: "/" },
  { name: "Interior Projects", path: "/interior-projects" },
]);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Interior Projects"
  description={`Residential, commercial and restoration interiors by ${SITE.name} across ${SITE.areaServed.join(", ")} and beyond.`}
  path="/interior-projects"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <section class="px-6 pt-16 pb-12 md:px-10 md:pt-24">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl">
      <p class="coord-mark">Interior Projects</p>
      <h1 class="mt-4 max-w-2xl font-display text-4xl text-ink md:text-6xl">
        Built interiors across Türkiye.
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-xl text-lg text-ink/75">
        {SITE.areaServed.join(" · ")}
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-t border-stone-light/70 bg-paper px-6 py-16 md:px-10 md:py-24">
    <div class="prose-content mx-auto max-w-3xl text-ink/85">
      <p class="coord-mark">Notes on memory</p>
      <p class="mt-6">
        One of the most common issues in luxury residential design is that the space appears
        overly flawless the moment it is completed. On paper, there is a strong composition, the
        right materials, balanced proportions, impressive lighting, and carefully selected
        furniture. In the renderings, everything is perfectly in place. The space looks
        aesthetically complete—yet it has not yet been lived in.
      </p>
      <p>
        What emerges at this stage is often not a true "place," but rather a well-designed space
        devoid of memory.
      </p>
      <p>
        A house transforms into a living space only when people begin to inhabit it. For a home
        is not merely an architectural shell. The way morning light falls on a particular
        armchair, the specific corner of a table where work is consistently done, the window by
        which coffee is enjoyed, the hallway where children run, and the spot where guests
        gather—these elements shape the character of the space over time. None of these
        behaviors can be fully replicated in a drawing.
      </p>
      <p>The designer creates the physical framework of the space, but it is the user who creates its memory.</p>
      <p>
        This is particularly significant in luxury design, where the concept of "perfection"
        often dominates high-budget projects. There is a desire for surfaces to remain
        unblemished, furniture to stay pristine, and materials to resist aging. Yet, it is
        precisely these traces that reveal a home has truly been lived in. Slight wear on a stone
        surface, the way a leather armchair molds to the body over time, tonal variations in the
        wood flooring, or the sheen that develops on a frequently touched door handle—these
        details make the space's history visible.
      </p>
      <p>These traces are not flaws; they are memories.</p>
      <p>
        A design that looks beautiful on paper encounters the real world for the first time only
        after execution. Scale, light, sound, materials, and the human body put the design's
        integrity to the test. Then, as the space is put to use, the design undergoes a second
        transformation. People use certain spaces differently than the designer envisioned; they
        rearrange furniture, introduce new objects, and adapt the setting to their own
        lifestyles.
      </p>
      <p>
        Therefore, a high-quality luxury residential design should not be a system that dictates
        every detail in advance. On the contrary, it should allow for the flexibility to let the
        user transform the space over time.
      </p>
      <p>
        Perhaps true luxury is not a home that looks flawless on day one, but rather a space
        that—even after a decade—bears the imprint of the people who live there, evolving while
        retaining its value.
      </p>
      <p>For the architect designs the space.</p>
      <p>But it is the people living within it who imbue that space with memory.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-t border-stone-light/70 px-6 py-12 md:px-10 md:py-20">
    <div class="mx-auto grid max-w-6xl gap-10 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
      {
        projects.map((project, i) => (
          <a href={`/interior-projects/${project.id}`} class="group block">
            <PlaceholderPlate
              label={`${project.data.title}, ${project.data.city}`}
              seed={i + 20}
              class="aspect-[4/5] w-full"
            />
            <p class="mt-4 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.1em] text-stone">
              {project.data.city} · {project.data.year} · {project.data.category}
            </p>
            <h2 class="mt-1 font-display text-xl text-ink group-hover:text-patina">
              {project.data.title}
            </h2>
          </a>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-t border-stone-light/70 bg-paper px-6 py-16 md:px-10 md:py-24">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl">
      <p class="coord-mark">Mood study</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 font-display text-3xl text-ink md:text-4xl">Mediterranean Atmosphere</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.1em] text-stone" lang="tr">
        Akdeniz Havası
      </p>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-xl text-ink/75">
        Travertine, alabaster and warm brass — a study in the material palette of a Mediterranean
        house.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-10 grid grid-cols-2 gap-4 md:grid-cols-4 md:auto-rows-[200px] md:grid-flow-dense md:gap-6">
        <img
          src="/images/interior-projects/mediterranean-atmosphere/living-room.jpg"
          alt="Warm Mediterranean-style living room with a travertine coffee table, bouclé sectional sofa and olive branches"
          class="col-span-2 aspect-[4/3] h-full w-full object-cover md:row-span-2 md:aspect-auto"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <img
          src="/images/interior-projects/mediterranean-atmosphere/kitchen.jpg"
          alt="Warm Mediterranean kitchen with a marble island and brass pendant lighting"
          class="aspect-[4/3] h-full w-full object-cover"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <img
          src="/images/interior-projects/mediterranean-atmosphere/bathroom.jpg"
          alt="Marble bathroom with a freestanding stone tub, olive tree and mountain view"
          class="aspect-[4/3] h-full w-full object-cover"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <img
          src="/images/interior-projects/mediterranean-atmosphere/bedroom.jpg"
          alt="Mediterranean-style bedroom with alabaster pendant lights and a sea view"
          class="col-span-2 aspect-[4/3] h-full w-full object-cover md:row-span-2 md:aspect-auto"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <img
          src="/images/interior-projects/mediterranean-atmosphere/bedside-vignette.jpg"
          alt="Bedside vignette with a travertine nightstand and alabaster pendant light"
          class="col-span-2 aspect-[4/3] h-full w-full object-cover md:col-span-1"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </div>

      <p class="mt-6 max-w-2xl font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.08em] text-stone">
        These images are AI-generated — a study of a memory-less space, not a built project.
        <span lang="tr" class="block normal-case not-italic tracking-normal">
          Bu görseller yapay zekâ ile üretilmiştir; hafızasız bir mekân tasarımı çalışmasıdır,
          uygulanmış bir proje değildir.
        </span>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .prose-content :global(p) {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .prose-content :global(p:first-child) {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
</style>
